WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.325
Here's the subtle one.

2
00:00:01.325 --> 00:00:03.722
Suppose a cloud network has both a private

3
00:00:03.722 --> 00:00:06.187
zone and a forwarding
rule for the same name.

4
00:00:06.187 --> 00:00:07.226
Which wins?

5
00:00:07.226 --> 00:00:08.975
The three clouds disagree.

6
00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:14.800
In AWS, the Route 53 Resolver rule
wins over the private hosted zone.

7
00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.602
In Azure, a linked private DNS zone
wins, and, per Microsoft Learn,

8
00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:23.388
the forwarding-ruleset
rule is silently ignored.

9
00:00:23.388 --> 00:00:28.174
In Oracle Cloud, the attached private
views are checked before resolver rules.

10
00:00:28.174 --> 00:00:32.900
Once a view answers, evaluation stops,
even if the answer is negative.

11
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.612
That word negative matters.

12
00:00:34.612 --> 00:00:36.650
A private zone can shadow a name.

13
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:41.280
If the zone covers a name but has no
record for it, it returns NXDOMAIN,

14
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.662
which means the name doesn't exist,
instead of asking anyone else.

15
00:00:45.662 --> 00:00:48.313
All three clouds document that behavior.

16
00:00:48.313 --> 00:00:50.949
So, as an inference, the same zone and the

17
00:00:50.949 --> 00:00:54.162
same rule give different
answers in each cloud.

18
00:00:54.162 --> 00:00:58.188
The only design that behaves the same
everywhere is one owner per name.

19
00:00:58.188 --> 00:01:00.351
The Grid owns agency zones.

20
00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:02.775
Each cloud owns its own private zones.

21
00:01:02.775 --> 00:01:04.887
Everything else forwards to the owner.

22
00:01:04.887 --> 00:01:08.509
Never put a zone and a rule for
the same name in one network,

23
00:01:08.509 --> 00:01:10.974
and test resolution from every origin,

24
00:01:10.974 --> 00:01:15.288
because a configuration that works
in one cloud can fail in the next.

25
00:01:15.288 --> 00:01:22.673
Sources: AWS, Route 53, Considerations
when working with a private hosted zone

26
00:01:22.673 --> 00:01:30.174
[S25]; Microsoft Learn, Troubleshoot
NXDOMAIN from an Azure Private DNS zone [S12

27
00:01:30.174 --> 00:01:33.521
(excerpt)]; Oracle, OCI Private DNS [S23];

28
00:01:33.521 --> 00:01:38.210
research brief, Bottom line
4 and failure mode F3.
